  "interpretation": "Gate 2 passed for all three connectome conditions: 125 of 125 rows each, no exception, illegal action, invalid ACTION6 coordinate, or budget violation, and a matching deterministic replay. That is engineering success: a valid agent that puts the complete 166,700-neuron graph in every decision.\n\nThe scientific result is negative. The real signed graph and the interface-matched no-recurrent-graph bypass scored exactly the same mean, 0.190476, each from exactly one game out of 25 - the fly solved sp80, the bypass solved r11l. This is the specification's section 19.3 cautionary pattern realized: a parameter-matched adapter that bypasses the graph matches it. The degree-preserving null scored zero, but so little separates these numbers that the difference rests on one game per condition and carries no statistical weight.\n\nAll 25 games produced identical action traces across all five seeds in every connectome condition, because the policy is deterministic and these environments are deterministic given the game. The effective sample size is 25 games, not 125 rows, which removes most of the power the paired design was assumed to have.\n\nBehaviorally the connectome agent explores less than chance: 52.8 unique observations per run against the random control's 64.2, a 34.8% observation repeat rate against 20.8%, and 23.7 consecutive repeats against 13.6. A deterministic argmax policy falls into loops that a random policy escapes. Thirty of 125 real-condition rows had action entropy below 0.2 bits.\n\nNo action trace was identical between any two graph conditions in any game, so the graph does causally determine behavior. It simply does not determine it better than a matched bypass does.",
